  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>Pain and difficulty chewing and speaking with loose, worn complete dentures that click and move; cause and treatment under evaluation with prosthodontics</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>K08.9</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting problem anchored to his documented painful dentures; superseded when the consultation made the diagnosis.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Complete edentulism of both jaws (American College of Prosthodontists diagnostic index class III: mandibular bone height about 14 mm, a flat lower ridge, a high muscle attachment), with complete dentures more than eight years old that no longer fit; synthetic modern diagnosis linked to his documented tooth loss from his twenties and his full dentures</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>K08.103</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis made at the prosthodontic consultation; the cause is recorded as unspecified because the sources do not establish why his teeth were lost.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Two shallow ulcers under the lower denture flange, probably traumatic from an ill-fitting denture, with no induration, red or white patch, or neck lymph node; to be re-examined two weeks after the denture is left out between meals</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>K12.1</td><td>S — Synthetic oral finding at intake; healed by the consultation, which confirmed denture-related traumatic ulcers.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-4">
    <td>Unintended weight loss of about 5.6 kg (6%) over six months from a narrowed, soft diet; at risk of malnutrition (MNA-SF 10)</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>R63.4</td><td>S — Synthetic nutrition problem linked to the documented difficulty eating with dentures.</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

